89 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 67 0 R
/T (Period poverty in Ireland is not a distant or isolated issue. Research and reports show that nearly 50 percent of girls have experienced some form of menstrual poverty. This includes struggling to afford period products, missing school days, or using unsuitable alternatives because there is no other choice. In practical terms, this means that in almost every classroom, there are girls quietly carrying a burden they should never have to bear.)
/E (Period poverty in Ireland is not a distant or isolated issue. Research and reports show that nearly 50 percent of girls have experienced some form of menstrual poverty. This includes struggling to afford period products, missing school days, or using unsuitable alternatives because there is no other choice. In practical terms, this means that in almost every classroom, there are girls quietly carrying a burden they should never have to bear.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
90 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 67 0 R
/T (For some girls, period poverty looks like staying home from school when their period arrives. For others, it means avoiding sports, school trips, or social activities. Over time, these interruptions affect confidence, participation, and academic engagement. What begins as a monthly challenge can grow into a long-term barrier.)
/E (For some girls, period poverty looks like staying home from school when their period arrives. For others, it means avoiding sports, school trips, or social activities. Over time, these interruptions affect confidence, participation, and academic engagement. What begins as a monthly challenge can grow into a long-term barrier.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 67 0 R
/T (ANUA starts by naming this reality honestly. Period poverty is not about personal failure. It is about access, dignity, and equality. If girls are expected to show up, learn, and succeed, then managing their period safely must be treated as a basic educational need, not an afterthought.)
/E (ANUA starts by naming this reality honestly. Period poverty is not about personal failure. It is about access, dignity, and equality. If girls are expected to show up, learn, and succeed, then managing their period safely must be treated as a basic educational need, not an afterthought.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
